/* ============================================================================
   power-regression.epher -- fit y = a * x^b to pendulum data

   Fits the least-squares power curve y = a * x^b to the pendulum periods
   in `ys` measured at the lengths in `xs` with the builtin powreg (guide
   1.20), which prints the fitted model and the correlation r of the
   log-transformed fit as a display string. Theory says the period goes
   as the square root of the length, T = 2 * pi * sqrt(L / g), so the
   fitted exponent should land near 0.5 and the coefficient near
   2 * pi / sqrt(g) = 2.0064. Both do. The data are illustrative.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/statistics/regression/power-regression.epher"
   In the REPL:             load epher scripts/statistics/regression/power-regression.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
   Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

   The knobs to edit are `xs` and `ys` below. Everything above the results
   section is machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs -----------------------------------------------------------------
const xs = {0.5, 0.8, 1.1, 1.4, 1.7}   // pendulum length, meters
const ys = {1.39, 1.82, 2.08, 2.41, 2.59}   // swing period, seconds

// ---- the fitted power curve --------------------------------------------------------------
powreg(xs, ys)      // least-squares power fit, printed with its r

// ---- the physics it recovers --------------------------------------------------------------
2 * pi / sqrt(g)    // the theoretical coefficient 2 * pi / sqrt(g), about 2.0064

/* ---- expected output ----
= {0.5, 0.8, 1.1, 1.4, 1.7}
= {1.39, 1.82, 2.08, 2.41, 2.59}
= y = 2.001*x^0.5105 (r = 0.9981)
= 2.00640929259
*/